data.content:
Short answer: not directly, no. `known-syntax-issues.json` only *patches* a token that already exists in the upstream IR — the generator only applies an entry when `name` **and** `upstreamValue` match a real token already produced by `buildTokens()`:

```ts
const known = knownSyntaxIssues.find(
  (k) => k.name === token.name && k.upstreamValue === token.value,
);
```

If no token with that name/value exists, the entry never fires.

The one loophole is `supplemental` — but it only adds a new token as a *side effect of a matching parent entry*, not standalone:

```json
{
  "name": "--instui-component-text-content-quote-font-weight",
  "upstreamValue": "Medium Italic",
  "rewriteValue": 500,
  "supplemental": [{ "name": "--instui-component-text-content-quote-font-style", "value": "italic" }]
}
```

So you can't add a truly net-new, unrelated token here without piggybacking it on some other real upstream bug — that'd be an abuse of the ledger, not the "official" path.

For a genuine net-new token in the IR, the official mechanism is a `PantokenPlugin`'s `tokens` hook via `buildTokens({ plugins: [...] })` — the same pattern `@pantoken/plugin-logos`, `-custom-icons`, `-simple-icons`, and `-primitives` all use:

```ts
import { buildTokens, type PantokenPlugin } from "@pantoken/core";

const myPlugin: PantokenPlugin = {
  name: "my-plugin",
  tokens: ({ tokens, define }) => [
    ...tokens,
    define({ name: "--instui-my-new-token", value: "..." }),
  ],
};
```

[formats/tokens/scripts/generate.ts](formats/tokens/scripts/generate.ts) currently calls `buildTokens({ theme })` with no plugins (just the deprecation shims + known-syntax patches applied after). Adding a genuinely new token to the *shared vendored IR* would mean passing a plugin there. If the token is scoped to just one component (like banner's `--pantoken-banner-*` internal custom properties), it doesn't need to go through the IR at all — declare it locally in the component's own CSS, same as the existing hand-rolled ones.

Yes, `"var(--instui-color-stroke-strong)"` is correct as-is — keep the full `var()` wrapper.

The matching check in `generate.ts` is a strict equality:

```ts
const known = knownSyntaxIssues.find(
  (k) => k.name === token.name && k.upstreamValue === token.value,
);
```

`token.value` for any Tokens Studio reference is produced by `resolveValue()` in [resolve.ts](packages/core/src/resolve.ts), which always emits the literal `var(...)`-wrapped string (not a bare custom-property name):

```ts
export function resolveValue(raw: string): string {
  const match = raw.match(REFERENCE);
  return match ? `var(${referenceToVarName(match[1])})` : raw.trim();
}
```

If you wrote the bare form (`"--instui-color-stroke-strong"`, no `var()`), it would never equal the actual `token.value`, so the entry would silently never match/fire — the patch just wouldn't apply and there'd be no error to tell you. Your entry is written correctly.
